Port of CrossOver's CW Hack 20969/23427 (+23394) to Wine 11.14: do not block the
painting thread on WM_NCPAINT, and bound WM_ERASEBKGND.

Strategic Command WWII (SDL 1.2, windib/GDI path) had pathologically laggy 2D
menus on macOS: a mouse-hover highlight took 5-10 seconds and menu transitions
tens of seconds, while the in-game map view was responsive. The same game under
CrossOver 26 is instant.

Measured before the fix: idle CPU ~25-30% and page faults ~0 in BOTH ours and
CrossOver, mach syscalls ~1,550/s vs ~1,450/s -- i.e. nearly identical. The only
delta was BSD syscalls, ~15,300/s vs ~7,100/s. So this was event/paint *latency*,
not throughput, drawing volume, or asset loading.

Ruled out along the way, each measured rather than argued:
  - the graphics path: no ddraw/opengl32/wined3d/d3d9/dxgi is ever loaded
  - asset loading: the rate is flat and interaction-independent
  - SDL's 1 ms multimedia timer: dlls/winmm/time.c is byte-identical to
    CrossOver's, and the mach-syscall rate (the timer tick) matches theirs
  - msync: CrossOver's bottle sets WINEMSYNC=1, but running their bottle with
    WINEMSYNC=0 left menus instant. NOTE: that control was not fully verified
    (the process environment was not confirmed), so msync is "not demonstrated"
    rather than "excluded".
  - CX HACK 23950 (shm surfaces): only fires for windows whose parent is in
    another process. Our game is single-process. See docs/NOTEBOOK.md -- it is,
    however, the fix for the separate Steam/--in-process-gpu problem.

The load-bearing change is the WM_NCPAINT one. send_message() blocks the caller
until the target replies; send_notify_message() does not. This is confirmed by
the ERR() kept in the WM_ERASEBKGND hunk: across a full launch and menu session
it fired ZERO times, so the 1s bound there never engaged and cannot be what
fixed anything. The ERASEBKGND hunk is carried anyway to match CrossOver and
because that ERR is the only diagnostic for this class of stall.

Verified: with this applied, menu highlighting and transitions are real-time.

Source: src/crossover/sources/wine/dlls/win32u/dce.c (CrossOver 26.0.0, LGPL).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
diff --git a/dlls/win32u/dce.c b/dlls/win32u/dce.c
index 5e8d82ae..f60cb9bb 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/dce.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/dce.c
@@ -1593,7 +1593,10 @@ static HRGN send_ncpaint( HWND hwnd, HWND *child, UINT *flags )
                 if (style & WS_VSCROLL)
                     set_standard_scroll_painted( hwnd, SB_VERT, FALSE );
 
-                send_message( hwnd, WM_NCPAINT, (WPARAM)whole_rgn, 0 );
+                /* CW Hack 20969, 23427: send WM_NCPAINT asynchronously. A
+                 * synchronous send blocks the painting thread until the target
+                 * replies, which on macOS can stall for seconds. */
+                send_notify_message( hwnd, WM_NCPAINT, (WPARAM)whole_rgn, 0, FALSE );
             }
             if (whole_rgn > (HRGN)1) NtGdiDeleteObjectApp( whole_rgn );
         }
@@ -1630,7 +1633,15 @@ static BOOL send_erase( HWND hwnd, UINT flags, HRGN client_rgn,
             {
                 /* don't erase if the clip box is empty */
                 if (type != NULLREGION)
-                    need_erase = !send_message( hwnd, WM_ERASEBKGND, (WPARAM)hdc, 0 );
+                {
+                    /* CW Hack 23394, 23427: bound WM_ERASEBKGND at 1s. An
+                     * unbounded synchronous send can stall for many seconds.
+                     * The ERR below is deliberately kept: it is the only signal
+                     * that this path is actually being hit. */
+                    need_erase = !send_message_timeout( hwnd, WM_ERASEBKGND, (WPARAM)hdc, 0,
+                                                       SMTO_ABORTIFHUNG, 1000, FALSE );
+                    if (need_erase && RtlGetLastWin32Error() == ERROR_TIMEOUT) ERR( "timeout.\n" );
+                }
             }
             if (!hdc_ret) release_dc( hwnd, hdc, TRUE );
         }
